+### 13.3 A guest under the runner is budgeted against the SCOPE, never against the box
+
+**`limits.memory_max` in `/etc/wrought/runner.conf` is the binding constraint on guest RAM, not
+host free memory.** The gate child runs inside a `systemd-run --scope` with `MemoryMax` **and**
+`MemorySwapMax=0`, and a guest it launches is a **descendant of that scope** — so the guest's
+memory is charged to the gate's own 8 G cgroup, and an overrun is an immediate OOM **kill of the
+gate**, not a slowdown. `MemorySwapMax=0` is exactly what makes it a kill rather than a swap-out
+(measured, `GATE-RUNNER-HARDEN raw/11`), so there is no soft landing here.
+
+Concretely, from `GATE-J0B-RESUME` (**F-2**, 2026-08-28): J0B's proven attended invocation is
+`qemu-system-x86_64 … -m 8192`. Under the runner that is **the whole scope**, and it would have
+OOM-killed the gate that launched it. The box had **84 GiB free at the time** — which is precisely
+why this needs writing down: every instinct and every `free -g` reading points the wrong way. The
+gate used **`-m 3072`** and completed.
+
+- **Current scope size: `limits.memory_max` = `8G`** (PROVISIONAL — the first supervised batch was
+  meant to set it, and has not yet been ratified at a ferry).
+- **Headroom a guest plus an agent needs:** the `claude` gate child itself, its tool subprocesses,
+  the QEMU process's own overhead above `-m`, and anything the gate builds all share that 8 G.
+  `-m 3072` is the only guest size measured to work inside it. Treat **guest `-m` + ~2 G** as the
+  floor for what the rest of the gate has left, and **do not raise a guest above `-m 4096` inside
+  an 8 G scope** without raising `memory_max` first — which is a ferry decision, not a gate's.
+- A gate that needs a bigger guest states so **in its prompt**, so the size is a reviewed number
+  rather than a discovery made by an OOM kill at 3 a.m.
